  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the resale value of a Maruti Swift in India?",
      "a": "A well-maintained Maruti Swift retains approximately 75% of its ex-showroom price after two years, 67% after three years, and 53% after five years, based on transaction data across Indian metros as of July 2026. For a 2022 VXi petrol with 25,000–50,000 km, the current fair-market band is roughly ₹4.8–5.53 lakh."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does a Swift CNG hold value better than a Swift petrol?",
      "a": "Yes, factory-fitted CNG Swift (2023 onwards) holds 3–5% more value than the equivalent petrol variant, particularly in Delhi NCR, Ahmedabad, and Mumbai. Aftermarket CNG kits fitted to older Swifts trade 6–10% below equivalent petrol, because of buyer concerns around kit approval and tank certification."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the 4th-generation Swift's 3-cylinder engine hurting resale?",
      "a": "Not materially, though the market has partly re-rated the prior-generation (2018–2024) K12N 4-cylinder Swift on the used market during the 4th generation's first year. As of mid-2026, a 2023 3rd-gen VXi with 10,000–25,000 km trades in a band that overlaps significantly with a 2024 4th-gen VXi at the same km, a compression that did not exist between the 2nd and 3rd generations."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much does a Swift depreciate per year?",
      "a": "The Maruti Swift depreciates roughly 15% in year one, an additional 10% in year two, then 7–8% per year for years three to five. The five-year retention of about 53% is 6–8 percentage points above the B-segment hatchback average."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which city gives the best resale price for a Swift?",
      "a": "Mumbai and Bengaluru currently trade at approximately +3% above the national mid, driven by tight used-car supply and strong hatchback demand. Kolkata trades at approximately −2%, and non-metro locations typically land 3–5% below the national mid depending on RTO and buyer pool."
    }
  ],
